  "live_records": {
    "q-54641": {
      "dom_id": "step-q54641",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Solve the equation $-{x}^{2}+2x=5.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange the equation to make the right-hand side equal to zero:\n\n$$\n-{x}^{2}+2x-5=0\n$$\n\nWe can find the roots of a quadratic equation using the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the coefficients are $a=-1,b=2,$ and $c=-5.$ Substituting these values into the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{{2}^{2}-4(-1)(-5)}}{2(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{4-20}}{-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{-16}}{-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\pm 4\\,\\text{i}}{-2} \\\\  & =1\\pm 2\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has two solutions, $x=1+2\\,\\text{i}$ and $x=1-2\\,\\text{i}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
