    "q-54642": {
      "dom_id": "step-q54642",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Solve the equation $-2{x}^{2}+4x-8=0.$",
      "worked_solution": "We can find the roots of a quadratic equation using the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the coefficients are $a=-2,b=4,$ and $c=-8.$ Substituting these values into the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{(4)^{2}-4(-2)(-8)}}{2(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{16-64}}{-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{-48}}{-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm 4\\sqrt{-3}}{-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm 4\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}}{-4} \\\\  & =1\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has two solutions, $x=1+\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}$ and $x=1-\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
