    "q-66426": {
      "dom_id": "step-q66426",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Solve  the equation $\\,-{x}^{2}-8x-41=0.$",
      "worked_solution": "We can find the roots of a quadratic equation using the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the coefficients are $a=-1,b=-8,$ and $c=-41.$ Substituting these values into the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-(-8)\\pm \\sqrt{(-8)^{2}-4(-1)(-41)}}{2(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pm \\sqrt{64-164}}{-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pm \\sqrt{-100}}{-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pm 10\\,\\text{i}}{-2} \\\\  & =-4\\pm 5\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has two solutions, $x=-4+5\\,\\text{i}$ and $x=-4-5\\,\\text{i}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-66426-a",
