      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "The equation ${x}^{2}+4x+8=0$ can be written in the form $(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q,$ and solve the equation.",
      "worked_solution": "We first move the constant to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4x+8 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4x & =-8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2}-{2}^{2} & =-8 \\\\ (x+2)^{2}-4 & =-8 \\\\ (x+2)^{2} & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=2,\\,q=-4,$ and $p+q=-2.$\n\nFinally, we solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ x+2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{-4} \\\\ x+2 & =\\pm 2\\,\\text{i} \\\\ x & =-2\\pm 2\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has two solutions, $x=-2+2\\,\\text{i}$ and $x=-2-2\\,\\text{i}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
